dicating genetic predisposition.", "GEMELOS MONOCIGOTICOS:Monozygotic twins have a significantly higher risk of developing Type 1 Diabetes if one twin is affected, highlighting genetic factors.", "HAPLOTIPO HLADR2:Haplotype HLADR2 is known to confer protection against the development of Type 1 Diabetes.", "FAMILIAS CAUCASICAS:Caucasian families were part of the genetic analysis conducted by the Type 1 Diabetes Genetics Consortium to study the disease.", "FAMILIAS ASIATICAS:Asian families were also included in the genetic analysis to understand the genetic factors associated with Type 1 Diabetes.)<|COMPLETE|>", "TYPE 1 DIABETES GENETICS CONSORTIUM:The Type 1 Diabetes Genetics Consortium is a group focused on studying the genetic factors associated with type 1 diabetes risk.", "HLA DR-DQ HAPLOTYPES:HLA DR-DQ haplotypes are genetic variations that have been studied for their association with type 1 diabetes risk.", "AIRE GENE:The AIRE gene is associated with autoimmune conditions, including type 1 diabetes, and is involved in immune tolerance.", "FOXP3 GENE:The FOXP3 gene is crucial for the development of regulatory T cells and is linked to autoimmune diabetes.", "POLYENDOCRINOPATÍA AUTOINMUNE DE TIPO 1:Polyendocrinopathy autoimmune type 1 (APECED) is a syndrome caused by a mutation in the AIRE gene, leading to multiple autoimmune conditions including diabetes.", "DIABETES MELLITUS TIPO 1:Diabetes Mellitus Tipo 1 (DM1) is a chronic autoimmune condition characterized by the destruction of insulin-producing beta cells in the pancreas, leading to little or no insulin production. This condition requires continuous and careful management of insulin and dietary intake to maintain healthy blood glucose levels. Patients with DM1 often face challenges related to poor glycemic control, which can complicate their overall health management. \n\nAdditionally, Diabetes Mellitus Tipo 1 is associated with a higher risk of developing eating disorders, particularly in young women. It frequently coexists with other autoimmune conditions, such as thyroid diseases and celiac disease. The patient has been managing this condition for 6 years, highlighting the long-term commitment required to effectively control the disease and mitigate its associated risks.", "CROMOSOMA 6P21:Chromosome 6p21 is the location of the major susceptibility locus for type 1 diabetes, containing genes related to the immune response.", "DIABETES MELLITUS TYPE 2:Diabetes Mellitus Type 2 (DM2) is a chronic condition characterized by insulin resistance and high blood sugar levels. It primarily affects the way the body processes blood sugar (glucose) and is often associated with obesity and other risk factors. This form of diabetes typically develops in adults and is marked by relative insulin deficiency. Management of Diabetes Mellitus Type 2 often involves lifestyle changes, including exercise and diet, and may also require medication. The condition is commonly linked to obesity, highlighting the importance of weight management in its prevention and treatment.", "GLUCEMIA PLASMÁTICA:GLUCEMIA PLASMÁTICA refers to the measurement of glucose levels in the blood, specifically in the plasma. This measurement is crucial for diagnosing conditions such as diabetes and prediabetes. Additionally, glucemia plasmática is utilized to evaluate carbohydrate metabolism, providing important insights into an individual's metabolic health."], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "temperature": 0.0, "frequency_penalty": null, "presence_penalty": null, "top_p": 1.0, "max_tokens": 4000, "n": 1}, "history": null}